{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Concrete RP-04 composition sketch added to .joe-janitor/rp04-secrets-research.md. The proposed seam is one immutable non-secret OperationSnapshot composed at runtime with separate GitRuntime, LLMRuntime, and selected BridgeRuntime capabilities; it is not a universal secret provider or credential union. Git delegates to credential helpers, SSH agents, or AWS-native remote helpers through a constrained runner environment. LLM provider-specific factories accept boto3, Anthropic, or OpenAI native auth capabilities while Rebar retains retry, timeout, cache, output-contract, and lifecycle policy. Each bridge factory accepts only its provider-specific client/auth shape; static Jira or Jenkins material is revealed only inside that adapter or its exact child environment. Operations instantiate only the runtimes they need, explicit auth selection fails closed without ambient or anonymous fallback, and lifecycle ownership follows client construction ownership. Open design decision: whether the public Python seam initially permits whole-client/runner replacement, which can bypass Rebar transport invariants, or only native auth-provider injection into Rebar-owned factories."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786936130850538001,"uuid":"e5860191-aacb-4f59-b19c-510828879633"}